    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The leading principality of north-eastern Rus’, ruled from Vladimir on the Klyazma after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> made it his capital in 1157.</li><li>Its white-stone churches and the icon of Our Lady of Vladimir became lasting symbols of Russian culture.</li><li>After the Mongol conquest of 1238 its grand-princely title was granted by the khans and finally passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The icon of Our Lady of Vladimir, painted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the early 12th century, was brought north by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> in 1155 and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a>’s Tretyakov Gallery.</p></aside>
    </section>

